## The work: closed a class by enumeration instead of sampling

Buckingham π on `F(ρ, |∇ρ|, ∇²ρ; G, a₀)` — 5 quantities, rank-3 dimensional matrix — gives **exactly two** groups. That turned a three-week-open search into a closed question testable all at once. On real SPARC (N=2614, 145 galaxies, no functional form/γ/ρ_crit/fitting): every group explains **≤0.16 %** of the RAR residual — *less than local ρ's 0.7 %*. `(g_bar, q)` = 0.1171 vs g_bar alone 0.1174: the shape group adds nothing to MOND.

Pre-empted rather than waited for: permutation null (q at z = −2.7, indistinguishable from its destroyed self), **flat at 1.34–1.40× across ϒ ∈ [0.30, 0.80]**, 16 estimator/window/scale-height/gas combinations, and 3rd derivative order.

Two things I didn't expect: the differential branch needed *fewer* parameters than the algebraic one it would replace (it genuinely deserved the execution it had been denied), and the degeneracy argument that was supposed to kill it **fails on real data** — `q` varies plenty (95.3 % within-galaxy). It dies because that real variation is uncorrelated with the boost, which is a cleaner kill leaning on no idealisation.

